  "name": "Ca 15.3-Breast Cancer Marker",
  "code": "CA15.3",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table><thead><tr><th><div>CA 15-3 Level (U/mL)</div></th><th><div>Interpretation</div></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><div>Normal Levels</div></td><td><div>Normal CA 15-3 levels in the blood are typically less than 30 U/mL, depending on the laboratory's reference range. Normal CA 15-3 levels are usually seen in healthy individuals with no evidence of cancer or other conditions that could cause CA 15-3 elevation.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Slightly Elevated Levels</div></td><td><div>Slightly elevated CA 15-3 levels (between 30 and 50 U/mL) may be seen in certain non-cancerous conditions, such as benign breast diseases or liver diseases. Additional evaluation may be needed to determine the cause of the elevation.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Moderately Elevated Levels</div></td><td><div>CA 15-3 levels moderately elevated (between 50 and 100 U/mL) may be associated with various cancers, including breast cancer, ovarian cancer, lung cancer, and others. However, other non-cancerous conditions can also cause moderately elevated CA 15-3 levels. Further testing, including imaging and other diagnostic procedures, may be necessary to confirm the diagnosis.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Markedly Elevated Levels</div></td><td><div>Markedly elevated CA 15-3 levels (above 100 U/mL) are more strongly associated with advanced or metastatic cancer. However, very high CA 15-3 levels can also be seen in non-cancerous conditions. Comprehensive evaluation and consultation with an oncologist are essential for a definitive diagnosis and treatment plan.</div></td></tr></tbody></table>",
